course: dictionary
lesson: 5388
title: Know: facts, people and skills
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Know is a small word with a big job. To know something is to have it in your mind as a fact, because you learned it or were told. «I know that seven eights are fifty-six» means you have that fact stored in your head.
explain: You can also know a person or a place. To know a person is to have met them and be familiar with them. To know a place is to be familiar with it. «Do you know the way home?» asks whether you are familiar with the route.
explain: You can know how to do something, which means you are able to do it. «She knows how to swim» means she can swim. Notice that know can be followed by a fact, a person, a place or a skill.
explain: Know changes its form: know, knows, knew, knowing, known. «Ella knew the way home» uses the past tense. Phrases like «I don't know», «you know» and «let me know» are used every day, so listen for them.
fact: Know is a verb, and its forms are know, knows, knew, knowing and known.
fact: To know something is to have it in your mind as a fact, because you learned it or were told.
fact: To know a person is to have met them and be familiar with them.
fact: To know how to do something is to be able to do it, as in «She knows how to swim».
fact: In a fair test, «you know which thing caused it» because you changed only one thing and kept everything else the same.
exercise: «She knows how to swim.» What does knows mean here? | She wants to swim | She is able to swim | She is afraid of the sea | *She has never seen water
exercise: In a fair test, why can you «know which thing caused it»? | Because the test is short | Because you wrote the answer down | Because you guessed | *Because you changed only one thing, so the change must come from that thing
exercise: «Ella knew the way home.» Find and copy the word that is the past tense of know. | known | *knew | knowing | knows
test: In «the last known one died in a zoo in 1936», what does known mean? | Very happy | *The last one anyone has proof of | Hidden in a cave | Famous and popular
test: Which phrase means you are familiar with a place? | *Know your way home | As far as I know | I don't know | Let me know
exam: In a maths question, the word know in «do you know how many…» asks you to | Guess a random number | Multiply the two numbers at once | Stop and go home | *Find the facts the question gives you before you calculate
